---
title: "Rust SDK"
description: "Parse, rewrite, plan, and execute QQL from Rust with qql-core and qql."
sidebar:
  order: 2
---

Use `qql-core` for the transport-free frontend and `qql` for execution.

{% terminal title="Install" %}
cargo add qql-core qql
{% /terminal %}

## Parse and enforce policy

{% qqlExample title="Input used below" %}
QUERY TEXT 'laptops' FROM products
USING dense
LIMIT 10;
{% /qqlExample %}

```rust
use qql_core::ast::{inject_filter, ComparisonOp, Value};
use qql_core::parser::Parser;

let mut stmt = Parser::parse(query)?;
inject_filter(
    &mut stmt,
    "tenant_id",
    ComparisonOp::Eq,
    Value::Str("org_99".into()),
)?;
stmt.set_shard_key(Some("org_99".into()));
```

## Execute

```rust
use qql::executor::{Executor, OnError};

let executor = Executor::rest("http://localhost:6333", None);
let report = executor.execute(query, OnError::Stop).await?;
assert!(report.ok);
```

For text input, configure an embedder or use precomputed vector input. The
runtime also provides `Executor::grpc`.

## Parser contracts

{% apiField name="Parser::parse" type="fn(&str) -> Result<Stmt, QqlError>" required=true %}
Parse one statement and require complete input consumption.
{% /apiField %}

{% apiField name="Parser::parse_all" type="fn(&str) -> Result<Vec<Stmt>, QqlError>" required=true %}
Parse one semicolon-delimited script.
{% /apiField %}
